# nvn/pso: this device's pso_user.tsv records every row the game asked for (crash-safe appends), and the prewarm walks it BEFORE the seed list (DM_PSO_USER_FIRST)
#
# Origin:  written in this repository after the Ocarina of Time (Switch 2, 0400486024DEC000) device session of 2026-11-20 (iPhone 15 Pro, 44 min, 9,022 frames). No upstream commit.
#          Applies after 0412 (patch -p1 --forward, no fuzz), to out-stage/out-engine/engine + engine-patches/0401..0412. iOSBuilder paches/0007, 0008, 0009 still apply on top of it and
#          land exactly where they did (their patched nvn_mtl.m differs from the 0412 one by this patch's lines only).
# Licence: the licence of the tree it patches (the engine's MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2026 seedds and contributors).
#
# ROOT CAUSE ("pso_user.tsv: 2 lines", the learned list that never grows)
#   The list is written, incrementally and on time: pso_user_record appends a row the moment the game needs a library (shader_get, the prefetch's claim, shader_prefetch_rest), a render
#   pipeline (pso_get, pull_pso, demand_record, bf_fn), a compute pipeline (dispatch_) or a mesh pipeline (mtk_pso), with fflush; the path is right (games/<id>/pso_user.tsv, the parent
#   of DM_SAVE; the log names it). It stays small BY DESIGN: a row the seed list (the pack's pso_manifest.tsv, 25,163 lines for this game) already holds is never written, so it is
#   "what the seed lacks", 2 rows after the first session. And the prewarm read [seed, user]: with DM_PSO_PREPARE_PERCENT=2 the head before the first frame was the seed's first 464
#   tasks by file order (464 library loads, 0 pipelines: "pipeline prepare: 464 lines (464 shaders, 0 render pipelines, 0 compute pipelines)") whatever this device had drawn, and the
#   one tail worker went on in file order: 5,506 of 22,727 tasks in 37 min, while the draws waited 930 s for compiles of what they needed (libraries 350 s, pipelines 580 s).
#   Two small weaknesses besides: fopen("a") + fputs + fflush leaves, after a kill in mid-row, a cut last row that the next start's first append glues to its own (one malformed line,
#   two rows lost); and no lock (every caller is on the submit thread today).
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES (DM_PSO_USER_FIRST, default on; =0 is exactly the old list and order)
#   * pso_user_record: the de-duplication set is this device's list only (not the seed): every row the game itself asked for on this device is written once, in the order it first
#     asked for it. Rows the prewarm built and the game merely claimed are not written (as before: warm_claim writes only the prefetch's). The file is bounded by the distinct rows the
#     game uses (at most the seed's size plus what it lacks: about 1-2 MB here).
#   * warm_paths: [this device's list, the seed] (was [seed, list]); warm_parse already drops a row met twice, so a seed row the list holds is built once, at the list's place. With the
#     split prepare step the head is then the first rows this device drew, then the seed's; the tail goes on with the rest of them, then the seed's. With DM_PSO_PREPARE_PERCENT=100
#     (the engine's default) every row is built before the first frame as before, in another order.
#   * Crash-safe appends: an O_RDWR|O_APPEND descriptor, one write(2) per row (a row is in the kernel when write returns: a jetsam kill or SIGKILL loses no row written, and two rows
#     never interleave), a line end written first when the file ends in a cut row, an os_unfair_lock around the writer. A row longer than the 8 KB buffer is not written (none is).
#   * Log: "pipeline prewarm: this device's list first, then the seed list (DM_PSO_USER_FIRST=0: the seed first)", and the cut-row line when it happens.
#   For the app: GameWarmLogic.NeededKeys (0061/0076) reads the keys of pso_manifest.tsv, then pso_user.tsv; it may read pso_user.tsv first to follow the host (not done here). An
#   exported player carries pso_user.tsv (0061): its first start's head becomes what the main app drew.
#
# MEASURED on this Mac (M1 Pro, macOS 27.3): dm/gfx/tests t_pso_user_first, 44 checks, 0 failed, with DM_PSO_MTL4=0 and =1: the rows (on / =0), the cut row, three writer processes
#   killed with SIGKILL mid-stream (150,000-180,000 rows in the runs, every one whole, none twice), eight threads writing at once (3,200 rows whole and once), warm_paths' three cases, warm_parse's
#   order, and the real prepare step on the GPU with DM_PSO_PREPARE_PERCENT=25: the head is exactly the 10 tasks the list names (on) or the seed's first 10 (=0), the tail builds the
#   rest once. t_pso_demand_record (its fclose of the old FILE * replaced by the descriptor's close) 54 / 40 / 51 checks as before; t_pso_head 348, t_pso_warm 123, t_pso_archive 432,
#   t_pso_prewarm 64, t_shmap_warm 12, unchanged.
# Mutations (target/agent-work/eng/tools/mutate.py): the seed first again fails 4 checks (then the test's alarm), no line end after a cut row fails 3.
# NOT VERIFIED (iPhone only): how much of what a session draws the next session's 2 % head and the tail then cover in time; the list's size after a long session; the app reading it.
# RISKS: the head of a split prepare step changes for every game with a list (it becomes the device's first-drawn rows): that is the point, but a list recorded with an old game version
#   is dropped row by row (warm_stale) as before. A device list from before this patch holds only rows the seed lacks: it is read first (its rows are what this device drew too).
# Roll back: DM_PSO_USER_FIRST=0 in env.txt (the old list and order), or remove this file from engine-patches/ and run scripts/build-host.sh again.

